hello and welcome to learning with  jagman x  today I'm going to be talking to you  about design terminology that will help  you communicate your ideas better  so if you are a designer or at least are  aspiring to be a designer then you might  have already heard that communication  skills are a necessity what's the point  in being able to dream up thousands of  design mechanics systems and other cool  ideas if you aren't then able to  communicate them to the rest of your  team or even to a publisher who might be  able to fund those ideas because  designers are often just seen as the  idea people which I guess is kind of  true but the best ones are the ones who  are able to directly and succinctly  explain how those ideas are going to  improve and experience and the clearest  way to do this is to use the correct  terminology to convey your message  almost four years ago I started working  as a designer within a triple A game  studio and it afforded me the chance to  work with my much much more experienced  designers and learn a lot about the way  they communicate with those around them  during these years I have collated a  list of the most useful terminology that  has helped me to communicate my design  ideas improvements or even my concerns  and this very list is what I would like  to share with you today so without  further Ado I will share with you my  list of super useful design terminology  affordances  affordances are simply put what an  object can do however this is only  revealed by the user taking the correct  possible actions so therefore it's up to  the designer to make sure that the  affordances are clear to be understood  by the user perceived affordances are  the affordances that the user perceives  that an object has if the designer has  done their job right then the perceived  affordances will line up with the true  affordances of the object and false  affordances are perceived affordances  that do not align with the object's True  affordances by either having a use that  was not intended or having no use at all  I'm going to use doors a lot this video  to give you examples of what I mean when  I'm talking about these design terms  because doors seem simple to design and  they are not trust me  if you know you know so the affordances  of a door is that a door can be opened  or closed pretty easy a perceived  affordance of a door would be that the  user sees that it has a handle and  therefore perceives that the door must  be pulled towards them to open a false  affordance of a door could be that the  user sees a key-sized hole underneath  the handle and perceives this door can  be locked with a key however this might  simply just be the design of the handle  and the door actually does not have any  locking capabilities it is a false  affordance when designing for games  however there are innate affordances  that players will expect that do not  necessarily align with real-world  experiences  for doors there is a rule of thumb that  usually they will open away from the  player for better ease of use signifiers  signifiers are specific elements of an  object that enhance its affordances  simply put  it shows what affordances something has  in our door example the signifier here  would be the handle as that is what the  users use to perceive what way the door  would open and also what action they  needed to take to open the door  this is a visual signifier as it's  something that the user sees to then be  able to perceive its affordances and  more simple examples of these would be  labels on jars or signs on roads to let  you know where to go in games we still  utilize this real world knowledge as it  keeps the environment familiar for the  player however the signifiers are  modified a little bit for the digital  platform for instance a door in a game  will often still have a door handle as  that is familiar to the player but we  will also need another signifier to show  them how to use the door and this is  often done with an on-screen button  prompt  Telegraph in game design the term  Telegraph can often be used alongside  the term signify because they both give  the user information about the object  however Telegraph is often used to give  advanced notice of a change to the  object rather than being intrinsic to  the object itself the term Telegraph  actually comes from Sports they use it  in martial arts to reveal what kind of  attack is coming next and it's also used  in games like chess where people try to  conceal what they are thinking so they  don't Telegraph to their opponent what  their next move is going to be and I can  relate to this because I do this when I  play battleships dad says my battleship  and history is me an example of this in  video games are wind-up animations to  Telegraph incoming attacks this gives  the player a chance to learn from them  and react to them as well  feedback  feedback is when the object reacts to a  user to let them know what action they  just took and lots of this time this  feedback can simply be the action  happening so if a user turns the handle  of a door and takes the action of either  pulling or pushing it to open the door  then the feedback would be for the door  to open if they took the correct action  and to stay closed if they took the  incorrect action however if the door  remains closed then how does this  feedback give information to the player  about why the door did not open if the  door remains closed a user can simply  look through the crack of the door to  see if it's locked or even give it a  shimmy and see if it has a bit of give  but in a video game players do not have  the same sensory information afforded to  them as users do in the real world so  the designers need to use a mix of  visuals and audio to help portray this  feedback lock doors for instance might  have a distinct locked noise or  sometimes even the player's character  might say something to them like I think  I need to find a key to open this door  feedback loops an extension of feedback  our feedback loops which are used to  either enhance or buffer changes within  your system there are actually feedback  loops all around you even now if you  decide to turn on your heating this  winter then that will cause a positive  feedback loop as your radiators will be  warming up but when they reach their  desired temperature the thermostat will  kick in and tell it to stop pumping out  Heat this is a negative feedback loop  and both negative and positive feedback  loops are needed to create balance in  games gameplay Loops are used to enhance  a player's gameplay experience by having  gameplay systems that are affected by  the player either negatively or  positively positive feedback loops  reinforce the current player experience  which allows the player to continue  either their successes or their failures  a famous example of positive feedback  loops is Call of Duty's killstreak  system this system basically gives perks  to players to enhance their success but  in turn this will also enhance the  failures of others negative feedback  loops however help to balance the  current player experience as they help  players who are struggling and hinder  players who are successful a famous  example of negative feedback loops in  games is Mario Kart's distribution  system which is weighted for the best  items to go to those who are further  back in the race this gives them more  chances to gain a blue shell which  specifically hinders the player in the  lead used well feedback loops keep the  game feeling fresh and adaptive while  also allowing it to be more accessible  to players of different skill levels as  the game can adapt its gameplay systems  to their playstyle rewarding those who  are skilled and also allowing them a bit  more challenge while helping those who  could be struggling  constraints constraints of rules that  are applied throughout the user  experience that either restraints or  enables that user constraints are really  important to allow the user to  understand the limits of the user  experience and therefore also allow them  to set their expectations and because of  this constraints are usually kept the  same throughout the whole experience in  video games these constraints can  manifest in many ways and the most  prominent example that I can think of is  full damage as a player myself I always  really like to play around trying to  find the limits of the full damage  before I see a death screen and although  as a rule of thumb it's always a really  good idea to keep your constraints  consistent throughout the experience to  keep in line with player expectations  games can sometimes allow a little bit  of Change by giving in-game item such as  upgrades or Buffs and designers  themselves must often understand their  own constraints when coming up with  designs and decision making constraints  are design limitations that designers  must work within the confines of however  these boundaries can often help to Aid  creativity as it gives designers  criteria that must be met and adapted to  and an obvious example of this is money  what really cool design idea can you  come up with within the confines of a  budget  constant  a constant is a rule that cannot be  changed and unlike constraints where  it's good practice to not change a  constraint constants are an ever-present  factor that can't be changed even if you  wanted it to gravity for example is a  constant it's something that's always  there and we can't change it but we work  within the boundaries of it in a game a  consonant can be the inability to see  beyond the screen it could be the need  for inputs or it could even be the  ability of the player themselves  States  objects and systems are made up of a  possible sequence of states and when  designing for an object it's really good  to understand what these possible States  could be for example a door could have  three possible States open closed and  locked  conditions conditions are rules that are  affected by user actions that meet  certain criteria that affect these  states for example the condition for a  door to be put into its open state will  be for the user to interact with it  however if the door is in a locked State  then the conditions will change so that  that same interaction will no longer be  able to open the door another action  will be needed  possibly unlocking death and damage are  common examples of conditions in video  games usually a player will have a  number that represents their health  but every time they get damaged this  number will get reduced now the  conditions for the death state to become  active will be if the number becomes  zero or less after being damaged  uniformity  uniformity is used to describe the need  for something to say consistent  throughout all types of an object and  this helps to make the object easily  identifiable to a user you often see  this in branding so that someone can see  an object and know exactly where it's  from but designers also use this to  build up perceived affordances within  users if all doors use uniformity to  show a panel to signify when a door  needs pushing and a handle to signify  when a door needs pulling there'd be so  much less confusion about what action a  user needs to take to open a door we've  all done it where you've pushed when it  was a poll or your pole when it was a  push it can be confusing at times games  use uniformity to help the player  understand the game world and roles  within it for example God of War uses  runes on pieces of the environment that  can be scaled climbed upon jumped off of  and Resident Evil uses the color yellow  on specific grapes that can be broken  and could contain loot  commonality commonality is quite similar  to uniformity however it's used more  throughout the development process to  explain the sharing of features or  attributes through different objects  where uniformity usually explains the  visual elements being shared commonality  usually explains the technical elements  being shared for example within a video  game you might have a wide array of  weapons that all might look quite  different but the most efficient way to  develop these weapons would be to ensure  that you had a commonality between the  setup of all of their shared features  such as melee bullet projectiles  reloading damage types to build your  games like this you might be interested  in looking into component based  designing  slow the term flow explains the intended  user experience when a user interacts  with an object from start to finish a  doors flow for example is for a user to  interact with it in a signified way  which allows the door to open then the  user can walk through to the other side  and it is up to them whether they would  like to close the door behind them in  video games however a doors design flow  is a lot more intricate as there are  many more factors to consider such as  whether a door can be locked or unlocked  what way it will open whether you have  NPCs and again that can interact with  the door if it's automatic or manual  here is an example of a really simple  door design flow that I wrote when  updating my indie game Perfection as you  can see writing out a flow graph can  really help you as a designer figure out  the flow of your designs as well as  catch any edge cases  Edge case  an edge case is a problem that occurs in  a very specific situation it's usually a  mild problem because of how specific it  is however it's always a really good  idea to try and anticipate where these  edge cases could be during the design  phase for example an edge case of an  audio speaker could be that at high  volumes the audio could distort  therefore lessening the overall quality  now some edge cases such as this one  might not be such an issue however some  of them would be worth addressing such  as if it's a specific system crash  exploit  exploits are potential or sometimes  specific flaws in a design that allows  the user to reach the outcome without  going through the intended flow for  example shaking a vending machine to get  to the yummy treats within the user  still gets a snack but they have missed  the vital part of having to insert money  first although I've used that as an  example but I'm pretty sure that only  works in the movies if you have done it  let me know in the comments maybe you  don't want people to know you've done  that actually  in game design the determination of what  is an exploit seems to differ but in my  experience I've seen many designers  design with exploits in mind for example  by creating gameplay systems and objects  within the world that hinder a player  but if used in a specific way can  actually help them as well a simple  example of this would be an exploding  red Barrel as players know to avoid them  because it could cause them damage  however they could also explode parts of  the environment nearby it exploiting the  area to the player's advantage while  also dealing damage to enemies nearby  and on the other spectrum of game  exploits are speedrunners which are  notorious for their exploitation skills  these players use glitches and bugs in  the game for example holes in a  collision to win the game in the  quickest way possible  salience  salience is an elegant word that  describes a part of the object's visual  design that's particularly notable if an  element seems to Garner the most  attention and jumps out from the  environment surrounding it then it's  Salient in game design especially level  design it's useful to think about this  in terms of where you need to lead the  player using interesting lighting or  notable environmental objects will  instantly draw player attention to that  location discoverability  discoverability is the degree of ease  that the user can find all of the  elements and features of a system when  they first encounter it the success of a  design usually depends on the amount of  discoverability that it has because  there's no point in coming up with a  really cool system or feature if a user  cannot discover its uses for themselves  keeping all of the terms that I have  described today in mind during the  design process will help you design  thoroughly and therefore enable you to  achieve a high level of discoverability  and that's it all of my most useful game  design terms congratulations for  reaching this far now you can go off and  tell the world your amazing design ideas  with your newfound communication skills  as a little disclaimer like a lot of the  human language a lot of these terms can  be used slightly differently by each  designer some designers have preferred  terms to others depending on their own  experience and specialisms however these  are the most common terms that I have  both heard in the workplace and read  about so I think they're the most widely  understood however if you think that you  know some that should be added in this  list but aren't then please do let us  know in the comments down below most of  these terms I actually learned from the  book The Design of Everyday things and I  have it here  this book  the Design of Everyday Things by Donald  A Norman this book was actually given to  me in the game Studio when I first  started out as a wee Diddy little game  designer so even though it is the Design  of Everyday things such as kettles and  doors it really taught me how to sync  like a designer it's a really insightful  book if you want to learn more about  design terminology as you can tell I've  gone off script a little bit now but I  did want to say if you wanted another  video of this that is specifically more  about game design terminology then also  do let me know in the comments and we  can share about things like weenies  because that's always a fun word to say  thank you so much if you reached this  far in the video it really means a lot  to me that you spend your time watching  my content I know it's been a while  since I made a Game Dev Style video so I  really hope you enjoyed this one I'm  currently working on a another music  track with my producer Jamie at Verizon  Productions and yes that will then  become Another music video made in a  game engine I also stream quite a bunch  on Twitch so if you want to watch me  play games talk about game design or  even join me on Monday mornings where I  do a Monday morning motivational stream  we talk about our weekly goals if that  sounds interesting to you then join me  on Twitch twitch.tv slash lagman X and  thank you so much to patreons I'll put  your name up on the screen now your  support really means so so much to me  and I always love chatting with you  especially in the Discord as well so  thank you very very much okay I think  that's enough from me have a lovely day  evening and I'll see you in the next  video bye
 

